4. Difference Between SSTF, SCAN and C-LOOK
Algorithm	Working
SSTF	Selects nearest request
SCAN	Moves to disk end and reverses
C-LOOK	Jumps between last and first pending requests

5. Important Functions and Variables
Name	Meaning
abs()	Calculates positive difference
RQ[]	Stores disk requests
initial	Current head position
TotalHeadMoment	Total head movement
index	Position of first greater request
move	Direction: 1 high, 0 low
size	Total disk size
temp	Temporary variable for swapping

6. Compile and Run Commands
SSTF
gcc sstf.c -o sstf
./sstf


SCAN
gcc scan.c -o scan
./scan


C-LOOK
gcc clook.c -o clook
./clook


Important: Remember These Three Concepts
- SSTF: Nearest request first.
- SCAN: Goes to disk end, then reverses.
- C-LOOK: Goes to last request, jumps to first pending request.
Note: The sample calculations above use the uploaded programs' logic with initial position 50, disk size 200 and high direction. SSTF does not use disk size or direction.












































